steps:
  - cmds:
      - view 1
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;Space, Time, and Energy}

      SW developers conventionally characterize their pro&shy;grams in terms of _time_ versus _space_ &ndash; a classic trade-off.

      But when targeting HW with limited power sources, we must also measure our programs&rsquo; _energy_ consumption.

      Consider `SleepyRunnerP`&thinsp;{[tr,102/07/03]} as a case in point:

      {[DC,1]} **EM&bull;Builder** itself reports code&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;data size [bytes]<br>
      {[DC,2]} **Logic 2** measures execution time [&mu;s] {[tr,102/08/02]}<br>
      {[DC,3]} **Joulescope Viewer** shows power over time [&mu;J]

      Note how {[dc,2]} and {[dc,3]} reveal different perspectives on the _same_ burst of active work &ndash; a single **CoreMark** iteration.

      <div class="em-info">

      The next tour will formally introduce [Joulescope](https://www.joulescope.com/).

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |
      {[ht,splitscreen]&thinsp;Side-by-Side Comparison}

      We can execute `SleepyRunnerP` on any target HW whose EMS distro package supports sleep&thinsp;+&thinsp;wakeup.&thinsp; {[tr,103/07/01]}

      Using the **Joulescope Viewer**, we've zoomed in on a single algorithmic burst running under three different distros.

      Compare the width and height of each active burst:

      burst {[dc,2]} &mdash; returns to sleep earliest with lowest avg mW <br>
      burst {[dc,1]} &mdash; takes longer to sleep with highest avg mW<br>
      burst {[dc,3]} &mdash; returns to sleep last but with good avg mW<br>

      Clearly, `nordic.nrf54` consumes the least amount of energy over this 20&thinsp;ms window of time.

      <div class="em-info">

      But what about `nordic.nrf52` versus `ti.cc23xx`&thinsp;??

      </div>

